<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Unattended install for Windows 11 ARM64 under qemu-system-aarch64 on Apple
  Silicon. Consumed by winvm.ts, which burns it onto the seed ISO.

  Everything in here was arrived at empirically against a real 25H2 ARM64 ISO.
  The comments record WHY, because every one of these was a failed install
  before it was a line of XML.

  ## No <DriverPaths>, on purpose

  UTM's own answer file injects viostor/vioscsi/vioserial at windowsPE. We do
  not, because we do not need to: the boot disk is NVMe, and Windows 11 ARM64
  has an in-box NVMe driver (stornvme.sys). UTM's wizard reaches the same
  conclusion — for aarch64 + Windows it hard-codes an NVMe interface, overriding
  the virtio default it uses for every other guest
  (Platform/Shared/VMWizardState.swift).

  This is not just tidiness. A <DriverPaths> entry names a DRIVE LETTER, and
  WinPE assigns letters by enumeration order across every attached CD. Hard
  -coding `E:` is a standing bet on device ordering — and UTM's own file loses
  that bet the moment you attach media in a different order. The drivers we
  actually want (vioserial for the guest agent, NetKVM for the NIC) are wanted
  AFTER the install, where a running OS can search for the volume instead of
  guessing. provision.ps1 does exactly that.

  ## Why the LabConfig bypasses are still here despite a real TPM

  swtpm gives this VM a genuine TPM 2.0 — the firmware log goes from
  `Tpm2SubmitCommand - Tcg2 - Not Found` to enumerating SHA1/256/384/512 PCR
  banks — so BypassTPMCheck is strictly redundant. BypassSecureBootCheck is NOT:
  Homebrew's QEMU ships only `edk2-aarch64-code.fd`, never the
  `edk2-aarch64-secure-code.fd` that UTM pairs with its TPM, so this firmware
  cannot present Secure Boot at all. The rest are kept because Setup reports one
  failure for the whole group; a redundant key costs nothing, a missing one
  costs a silent stall with no console attached.
-->
<unattend xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:unattend">

  <settings pass="windowsPE">
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core-WinPE"
               processorArchitecture="arm64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">
      <SetupUILanguage><UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage></SetupUILanguage>
      <InputLocale>en-US</InputLocale>
      <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
      <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
      <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Setup"
               processorArchitecture="arm64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">

      <RunSynchronous>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\SYSTEM\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassTPMCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>2</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\SYSTEM\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassSecureBootCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>3</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\SYSTEM\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassRAMCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>4</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\SYSTEM\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassCPUCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
      </RunSynchronous>

      <!--
        WillWipeDisk is deliberate and destructive. The target is a qcow2 that
        `winvm.ts init` creates fresh; an install that half-inherits a previous
        layout is worse than one that refuses to start.
      -->
      <DiskConfiguration>
        <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        <Disk wcm:action="add">
          <DiskID>0</DiskID>
          <WillWipeDisk>true</WillWipeDisk>
          <CreatePartitions>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>1</Order><Type>EFI</Type><Size>260</Size>
            </CreatePartition>
            <!-- Microsoft Reserved: no filesystem, no letter, required on GPT. -->
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>2</Order><Type>MSR</Type><Size>128</Size>
            </CreatePartition>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>3</Order><Type>Primary</Type><Extend>true</Extend>
            </CreatePartition>
          </CreatePartitions>
          <ModifyPartitions>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>1</Order><PartitionID>1</PartitionID><Label>System</Label><Format>FAT32</Format>
            </ModifyPartition>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>2</Order><PartitionID>2</PartitionID>
            </ModifyPartition>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>3</Order><PartitionID>3</PartitionID><Label>Windows</Label>
              <Letter>C</Letter><Format>NTFS</Format>
            </ModifyPartition>
          </ModifyPartitions>
        </Disk>
      </DiskConfiguration>

      <ImageInstall>
        <OSImage>
          <!--
            BY NAME, not by index. The retail multi-edition ARM64 ISO carries
            three images — verified by parsing the WIM header's XML resource:
              1 Windows 11 Home / 2 Home Single Language / 3 Pro
            Index order is a property of one ISO build. The name is stable.
          -->
          <InstallFrom>
            <MetaData wcm:action="add">
              <Key>/IMAGE/NAME</Key>
              <Value>Windows 11 Pro</Value>
            </MetaData>
          </InstallFrom>
          <InstallTo><DiskID>0</DiskID><PartitionID>3</PartitionID></InstallTo>
        </OSImage>
      </ImageInstall>

      <UserData>
        <AcceptEula>true</AcceptEula>
        <FullName>winvm</FullName>
        <Organization>winvm</Organization>
        <!--
          Microsoft's published generic Windows 11 Pro key. It selects the
          edition and suppresses the key prompt; it does NOT activate. This guest
          runs as unactivated Windows, which is permitted indefinitely and is the
          right posture for a disposable fixture.
        -->
        <ProductKey>
          <Key>VK7JG-NPHTM-C97JM-9MPGT-3V66T</Key>
          <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        </ProductKey>
      </UserData>
    </component>
  </settings>

  <settings pass="specialize">
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup"
               processorArchitecture="arm64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">
      <ComputerName>WINVM</ComputerName>
      <TimeZone>UTC</TimeZone>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Deployment"
               processorArchitecture="arm64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">
      <RunSynchronous>
        <!--
          OOBE's "let's get you connected" page is otherwise unskippable without
          a Microsoft account, and this guest has no working NIC until the guest
          tools land at first logon — so setup would deadlock waiting for a
          network it cannot have.
        -->
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\OOBE /v BypassNRO /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <!--
          UAC OFF, and this one is load-bearing rather than a convenience.
          FirstLogonCommands run as the auto-logged-on administrator, but with
          UAC on they run UNELEVATED: the guest-tools installer then raises a
          consent dialog that no one is there to answer, and Start-Process
          reports `The operation was canceled by the user`. Worse, the UAC
          dialog focuses "No" by default, so anything blindly sending Enter
          actively DENIES it.

          This also moves the guest closer to CI: GitHub's Windows runners run
          with admin rights and UAC disabled. Appropriate for a throwaway
          fixture on a loopback NIC; do not copy this onto a real machine.
        -->
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>2</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\System /v EnableLUA /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <!--
          Stage provisioning as SetupComplete.cmd, which Windows runs at the end
          of Setup as SYSTEM, before any logon.
          FirstLogonCommands is NOT used: it needs a user session, runs
          unelevated (so the guest-tools installer raises a UAC dialog nobody
          can answer), and is silently suppressed on 25H2 by the deprecated
          Skip*OOBE settings. Every one of those failures is invisible — the
          desktop appears and nothing runs.

          The drive letter is searched rather than assumed: WinPE and the
          installed OS enumerate removable media differently, and the seed's
          letter is not stable between them.
        -->
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>3</Order>
          <Path>cmd /c md C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>4</Order>
          <Path>cmd /c for %d in (D E F G H I) do @if exist %d:\SetupComplete.cmd copy /y %d:\SetupComplete.cmd C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>5</Order>
          <Path>cmd /c for %d in (D E F G H I) do @if exist %d:\provision.ps1 copy /y %d:\provision.ps1 C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
      </RunSynchronous>
    </component>
  </settings>

  <!--
    PROVISIONING RUNS HERE, because the guest boots to audit mode (see the
    oobeSystem pass). Microsoft names this pass for exactly this job: "Add
    Microsoft-Windows-Deployment\RunAsynchronousCommand or RunSynchronousCommand
    to the auditUser configuration pass to run a script that runs when the PC
    enters audit mode. This can be helpful for tasks like automated app
    installation or testing."
    <https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/manufacture/desktop/add-a-custom-script-to-windows-setup>

    `SetupComplete.cmd` still runs too, and still invokes the same script. Both
    entry points are kept because the script is idempotent and returns
    immediately once it has completed once — whichever fires first does the
    work. That is deliberate belt-and-braces: SetupComplete.cmd is the
    documented once-ever hook, and this pass is the documented audit-mode hook.

    The path is absolute rather than searched: the specialize pass above has
    already copied provision.ps1 to C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts.
  -->
  <settings pass="auditUser">
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Deployment"
               processorArchitecture="arm64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">
      <RunSynchronous>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <Path>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\provision.ps1</Path>
          <Description>winvm provisioning (idempotent)</Description>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
      </RunSynchronous>
    </component>
  </settings>

  <settings pass="oobeSystem">
    <!--
      ================= THE MOST IMPORTANT SETTING IN THIS FILE =================

      AUDIT MODE, NOT OOBE. This one setting is what makes the guest boot
      straight to a logged-on desktop, every time, with no out-of-box
      experience at all.

      Microsoft: "Booting to audit mode starts the computer in the built-in
      administrator account", and "After you configure a computer to boot to
      audit mode, the computer will continue to boot to audit mode by default
      until you configure the computer to boot to OOBE."
      <https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/manufacture/desktop/boot-windows-to-audit-mode-or-oobe>

      WHY IT IS NOT OPTIONAL HERE. With OOBE, every fresh instance runs the
      out-of-box experience on its first boot: it logs on a temporary account
      called `defaultuser0`, sits on "Just a moment, checking for updates" for
      minutes against the network, then reboots. Three consequences, all
      observed:

        - There is no reliable moment to call the guest "ready". qemu-ga runs as
          SYSTEM and answers throughout OOBE; so does guest-exec; and Windows
          reports OOBEInProgress=0, SystemSetupInProgress=0 and a running
          explorer.exe while the OOBE screen is still on display. Every probe
          says yes too early.
        - Snapshotting during that window LIVELOCKS the guest — all vCPUs at
          100%, no disk I/O, recoverable only by killing it.
        - The post-OOBE reboot is itself a failure point; one hung at the
          firmware splash burning 100% CPU indefinitely.

      Audit mode removes the phase rather than tolerating it. Per the Reseal
      table, oobeSystem + Mode=Audit + ForceShutdownNow=false means: ignore
      every other setting in this pass, then start the auditSystem pass.
      <https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/desktop/unattend/microsoft-windows-deployment-reseal-mode>

      SO EVERYTHING BELOW IN THIS PASS IS INERT while this is set. It is kept,
      not deleted, because each entry records a real failure that would return
      the moment anyone switches back to OOBE.
    -->
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Deployment"
               processorArchitecture="arm64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">
      <!--
        ELEMENT ORDER MATCHES MICROSOFT'S OWN XML EXAMPLE — ForceShutdownNow
        before Mode. Unattend files are schema-validated and these are ordered
        sequences, so a reversed pair risks the component being rejected whole.

        ForceShutdownNow MUST be false here: the docs mark oobeSystem + Audit +
        ForceShutdownNow=true as "Unsupported. Do not use. Sets the computer into
        a loop of reboots."
      -->
      <Reseal>
        <ForceShutdownNow>false</ForceShutdownNow>
        <Mode>Audit</Mode>
      </Reseal>
    </component>

    <!--
      NOTHING ELSE BELONGS IN THIS PASS.
      =================================

      The OOBE components that used to live here — Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup
      with UserAccounts, AutoLogon, OOBE screen-hiding and FirstLogonCommands,
      plus Microsoft-Windows-International-Core — were removed, and their absence
      is load-bearing.

      They were kept once on the strength of the documented line that oobeSystem
      settings "do not appear in audit mode". THEY ARE NOT INERT. Windows honoured
      the audit-mode request (`IMAGE_STATE_SPECIALIZE_RESEAL_TO_AUDIT`) and
      enabled the built-in Administrator, and then AutoLogon signed in `winvm`
      instead. audit.exe had arranged to be re-called as `audit.exe /user` on the
      AUDIT logon, which therefore never happened — so the auditUser pass never
      ran, provisioning never ran, and the guest sat idle on a desktop with no
      agent and nothing anywhere saying why.

      Audit mode logs on the built-in Administrator by itself. Give it no other
      account to prefer.
    -->
  </settings>
</unattend>
